# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This page explains how the GOV.UK Content API represents GOV.UK content and
describes the data it returns.

<%= partial "partials/content_api_def" %>

## What the GOV.UK Content API returns

A successful request to the GOV.UK Content API returns a JSON response representing
a piece of content, conforming to the [`ContentItem`][content-item] schema.

You can retrieve this data for a supported GOV.UK page by including the page's
path in a request to the GOV.UK Content API. For example, for the [VAT rates](https://www.gov.uk/vat-rates)
page:

```shell
curl https://www.gov.uk/api/content/vat-rates
```

The response contains fields for the content itself, its metadata and its relationships
with other GOV.UK content.

The following sections explain the purpose of the key fields in the response
and what their values mean.

### Base paths

```json
{
  ...
  "base_path": "/vat-rates",
  ...
}
```

Every successful API response has a `base_path` field. This field always has the
same value as the [path parameter][path-get-parameters] used to identify the content
in the API request.

This is called the _base_ path because it is the canonical path to the piece
of content. Some pieces of content may appear on [multiple pages](#canonical-paths-and-redirects)
on the GOV.UK website.

### Document types and schema names

```json
{
  ...
  "document_type": "answer",
  "schema_name": "answer",
  ...
}
```

The `document_type` and `schema_name` fields are used to describe the type and
structure of data returned for a piece of content:

- `document_type`: specifies the type of content represented by the response.
  This field typically indicates how the piece of content is used on the GOV.UK
  website.
- `schema_name`: refers to a [JSON schema][json-schema] file held in
  [GOV.UK Content Schemas][content-schemas-repo].
  This schema defines the structure of the object in the response's `details`
  field.

Often the `document_type` and `schema_name` fields share the same value. In the
[VAT rates][vat-rates-api] example, both fields have value `answer`.

The `document_type` and `schema_name` can also have different values, because
multiple document types can conform to the same schema. This allows pieces of content
with the same data structure to serve different purposes on GOV.UK.

The values of `document_type` and `schema_name` affect the [`details`](#details)
and [`links`](#links) fields. The following sections explain these effects.

### Details

<!-- markdownlint-disable MD013 -->
```json
{
  ...
  "details": {
    "body": "\n<div class=\"highlight-answer\">\n<p>The standard <abbr title=\"Value Added Tax\">VAT</abbr> rate...",
    "external_related_links": []
  },
  ...
}
```
<!-- markdownlint-enable MD013 -->

(The `body` in this example has been shortened for presentation here.)

The `details` field in an API response is an object that contains data in an appropriate
structure for the piece of content. This structure is defined by the [JSON schema](#document-types-and-schema-names)
identified in the `schema_name` field.

In the [VAT rates][vat-rates-api] example, the [`answer`][answer-schema] schema
is used. This `answer` schema specifies the following for the `details` field:

- a `body` field, which contains the HTML for the main body of the piece of content.
- an `external_related_links` field containing links to related web pages outside
  the GOV.UK website.

### Links

```json
{
  ...
  "links": {
    ...
      "organisations": [
        {
          "analytics_identifier": "D25",
          "api_path": "/api/content/government/organisations/hm-revenue-customs",
          "api_url": "https://www.gov.uk/api/content/government/organisations/hm-revenue-customs",
          "base_path": "/government/organisations/hm-revenue-customs",
          "content_id": "6667cce2-e809-4e21-ae09-cb0bdc1ddda3",
          "details": {
            "acronym": "HMRC",
            "brand": "hm-revenue-customs",
            ...
            "organisation_govuk_status": {
              "status": "live",
              "updated_at": null,
              "url": null
            }
          },
          "document_type": "organisation",
          "links": {},
          "locale": "en",
          "schema_name": "organisation",
          "title": "HM Revenue & Customs",
          "web_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/hm-revenue-customs",
          "withdrawn": false
        }
      ],
    ...
  },
  ...
}
```

The `links` field in an API response is used to describe relationships between
the piece of content represented, and other GOV.UK content. This field is an object
in which each key identifies a type of content relationship.

The content [schema](#document-types-and-schema-names) identified by the
`schema_name` field defines which link types can appear as keys in the
`links` object. The value associated with each key is an array of objects
representing related pieces of content of that type.

Each object in the array conforms to the [`LinkedContentItem`][linked-content-item]
schema, containing the fields needed to express the content relationship. This
may itself include a `links` field, allowing content relationships to be nested.
You can use nested `links` fields and the `parent` link type to show a hierarchy
of content in your application or service.

Although these relationships are called _links_, they are not necessarily presented
as HTML links when the content is rendered on the GOV.UK website.

For example, in the [VAT rates][vat-rates-api] response, the `organisations` link
type identifies the government organisations related to the piece of content. You
can use this data to retrieve other content published by those organisations,
such as news or guidance.

## HTML in responses

Some fields in the API response, such as the `body` of the `details` object, may
contain content formatted using [Govspeak][govspeak]. Govspeak is a simplified
Markdown syntax developed for GOV.UK.

The GOV.UK Content API converts this Govspeak into HTML before returning it in
the response. This means you can render the content in your application with its
intended structure, such as headings, lists and links, without needing
to implement your own Govspeak parser.

The API response does not return CSS with this HTML, so you must provide the CSS
needed to style the content in your application or service.

## Content states and paths

### Canonical paths and redirects

A single piece of content served by the GOV.UK Content API can be used to render
multiple pages on the GOV.UK website. For example, the [local laws and customs]
[thailand-laws-customs] for Thailand are available as a standalone page, and also
as one part of a [printable guide][thailand-print].

The canonical path to the piece of content for both of these pages is
[/foreign-travel-advice/thailand][thailand-api], and you can retrieve this content
using the GOV.UK Content API.

If you request this piece of content using the path to the standalone page
`/foreign-travel-advice/thailand/local-laws-and-customs`, the API returns a 303
`HTTP` status code redirecting you to the canonical path.

```shell
$ curl -I https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/thailand/local-laws-and-customs
HTTP/1.1 303 See Other
...
Location: https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/thailand
...
```

## Withdrawn content

<!-- markdownlint-disable MD013 -->
```json
{
  ...
  "withdrawn_notice": {
  "explanation": "<div class=\"govspeak\"><p>This document is out of date.  See the current <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-complete-routine-immunisation-schedule\">complete routine immunisation schedule</a>.</p>\n</div>",
  "withdrawn_at": "2015-08-12T13:47:11Z"
  }
  ...
}

```
<!-- markdownlint-enable MD013 -->

Some content on the GOV.UK website provides information that is no longer
applicable, but remains available for reference. This content is described
as _withdrawn_.

Withdrawn content is displayed on the GOV.UK website with a header describing
its status. For example,
[The complete routine immunisation schedule 2013 to 2014][immunisation-withdrawn]
was withdrawn because the content became out of date.

In a response from the GOV.UK Content API for a piece of withdrawn content, this
status is indicated using the [`withdrawn_notice`][withdrawn-notice] field.

